You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: int_workday__organization_metrics
What the agent's work assumed: 平均任期只用当前任职来算就行
What was actually required or true: 用全部任职（历史加当前）来算

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Average tenure is calculated only over current assignments instead of all historical and current assignments.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "FROM assignments WHERE effective_end IS NULL GROUP BY organization_id,source_relation",
      "source": "avg_tenure_days ... across all assignments; defaults to 0",
      "reason": "With no current assignments, every organization's tenure defaults to zero despite available historical tenure."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Weekly-hours aggregates are computed after job-family and family-group expansion, potentially counting the same current position multiple times.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "sum(coalesce(default_weekly_hours,40)) ... FROM assignments",
      "source": "Average weekly hours over current positions ... Total weekly hours over current positions",
      "reason": "One-to-many hierarchy relationships can inflate hours and reweight averages, though current assignments are absent in this input."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Grade frequency is measured after family and group joins, so hierarchy multiplicity may determine most_common_grade.",
      "about": "data",
      "evidence_in_work": "order by count(*) desc, compensation_grade_id asc ... FROM assignments",
      "source": "Most frequent compensation grade across current positions",
      "reason": "Grades linked to more hierarchy rows could win regardless of their actual position frequency."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Transfer-out detection requires a later assignment to start after the old assignment ends, which may miss later timeline assignments that overlap.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "x.effective_start>a.effective_end",
      "source": "had any later assignment in a different organization within the same source",
      "reason": "The interpretation of later assignment was not validated and can change mobility counts for overlapping histories."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Last-year mobility filters have no upper bound and can count future assignment starts or ends.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "effective_end >= current_date - INTERVAL '1 year'",
      "source": "assignment ended in the past year",
      "reason": "Assignment staging does not exclude future dates, so future events may enter retrospective metrics."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The initial position join omits source_relation and may cross-match positions across sources.",
      "about": "data",
      "evidence_in_work": "ON wpo.position_id=p.position_id",
      "source": "grouped by organization and source",
      "reason": "All generated staging rows currently use one source label, making this a low-likelihood but potentially broad join issue."
    }
  }
]
